2.2 Event Sequencer (Core Dispatcher)

Purpose: Serialize all events (market data + algo actions) into a single, timestamp-ordered stream. This is the heartbeat of the simulator.

Aspect Design
Pattern LMAX Disruptor-style ring buffer (lock-free, pre-allocated)
Java 11 impl com.lmax:disruptor (Java 11 compatible) OR hand-rolled AtomicLong-based ring
Event types MarketDataEvent, OrderActionEvent, FillEvent, TimerEvent, ControlEvent
Ordering Strict timestamp order; ties broken by priority (market data > algo decision > algo action)
Determinism Same input stream → identical output sequence. No System.nanoTime() in logic — time is an input field on every event 
Backpressure Bounded ring; if consumer lags, producer blocks (deterministic, not lossy)

Why Disruptor-style: Lock-free, zero-allocation on the hot path, and deterministic ordering. Java's ArrayBlockingQueue introduces lock contention and GC pressure .

Java 11 constraints:

· No var in lambdas (Java 10+ has it, but keep code style consistent).
· Use java.util.concurrent.atomic primitives carefully — AtomicLong with lazySet/getAcquire (Java 9+) is fine.
· Avoid CompletableFuture in the hot path — it allocates.

---

2.3 Matching Engine (Queue-Aware)

Purpose: Simulate venue behavior: price-time priority, partial fills, queue position, latency, halts.

Component Responsibility
Order Book Per-symbol L3 book: price levels → FIFO queues of resting orders
Queue Tracker Tracks position of every resting order (including algo's iceberg slices) at each price level
Matching Logic Price-time priority; partial fills; residual rests for GTC, cancels for IOC/FOK
Latency Model Configurable: deterministic (fixed delay) or stochastic (Gaussian/log-normal) 
Halt Model Market halt states: REJECT_NEW, DELAY, FULL, PARTIAL 
Adverse Selection Model When a fill occurs, sample whether it correlates with subsequent adverse price move

Queue position is the critical realism factor. Naive simulators assume "touch = fill." This simulator tracks: how many shares are ahead of you at your price level, and only fills when cumulative executed volume at that level exceeds that amount .

Java 11 data structures:

· Price levels: TreeMap<Long, PriceLevel> (long = scaled integer price) — O(log n) insert, O(1) best-price via firstEntry()/lastEntry().
· FIFO queue per level: ArrayDeque<OrderNode> — pre-allocated nodes from an object pool.
· Order lookup: HashMap<Long, OrderNode> (order ID → node) for O(1) cancel.
· Object pooling: Custom OrderNodePool to avoid GC churn on the hot path .

Fixed-point arithmetic: Use long with 10^8 scale for prices and quantities. No double, no BigDecimal on the hot path .

