3. Java 11 Implementation Notes

3.1 Determinism Rules

1. Time is an input, not a call. Every event carries a timestamp. Never call System.nanoTime() in algo logic .
2. No HashMap iteration order dependence. Use LinkedHashMap or TreeMap where order matters.
3. No floating-point on the hot path. Use long scaled integers.
4. Seed all randomness. java.util.Random with a fixed seed; never Math.random().
5. No external I/O in the hot path. Tick DB reads happen in the replay thread; algo logic is pure.

3.2 Memory Management

· Object pooling: Pre-allocate OrderNode, MarketDataEvent, FillEvent objects. Reuse via a free-list .
· Off-heap storage: For large order books, consider ByteBuffer.allocateDirect().
· GC tuning: Use G1GC; target < 1 GB/hour allocation rate; avoid finalize() .
· Ring buffer sizing: Power of 2; e.g., 8192 or 16384 slots .

3.3 Threading Model

Thread Responsibility
Replay Thread Reads tick DB, publishes MarketDataEvent to ring buffer
Algo Thread Consumes events, runs algo logic, publishes OrderActionEvent
Matching Thread Consumes order actions, runs matching engine, publishes FillEvent
Log Writer Thread Consumes event log stream, writes to disk
Analysis Threads Run metrics/detection offline (post-run)

Inter-thread communication: SPSC ring buffers (one producer, one consumer per stage). No shared mutable state .

